#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""aq-approve-headless — Approval Control Plane P4 headless/rescue authorization CLI.

Implements `.agents/plans/approval-control-plane/ACP-P4-DESIGN-20260816.md`: lets the owner
authorize a critical `aq.approval-request.v1` action from a console with NO browser (SSH
session, headless VT, NixOS recovery shell) using the SAME security guarantees as the P2 web
path. Predecessor: `scripts/ai/lib/approval_signer.py` (P1) — this CLI drives the IDENTICAL
`ApprovalSigner.begin_challenge` / `ApprovalSigner.sign_request` methods P1 already built and
P2's dashboard route (`dashboard/backend/api/routes/approvals.py`) already wraps for the
browser path. P4 changes only WHERE the human-factor ceremony runs (a local hidraw FIDO2
client instead of `navigator.credentials.get()` in a browser) — never the authorization
semantics: WYSIWYS, request_id-bound single-use, fail-closed, no-downgrade all hold unchanged.

NAMING NOTE: `scripts/ai/aq-approve` already exists and is WIRED (nix/modules/roles/ai-stack.nix
`writeShellScriptBin`, referenced by `aq-alerts`/`aq-chat`, gated by
`scripts/testing/harness_qa/phases/phase0.py` check 86.5) for a DIFFERENT, unrelated purpose —
Phase 86 `human_gate` alert approval (`<alert-id>`, attention_queue-backed). That is a live
production tool; this build does not touch it. This ACP-P4 CLI ships as `aq-approve-headless`
instead. See `.agents/plans/approval-control-plane/ACP-P4-BUILD-20260817.md` for the full note;
renaming the design doc's chosen name to avoid the collision (or reconciling the two tools) is a
decision for the orchestrator/owner, not made unilaterally here. Nothing here is wired into
`ai-stack.nix`/QA yet either way (Activation Gate: build-only, default-OFF).

Human-factor ceremony (design "Design" step 1-3):
  1. `list` — enumerate pending requests in PLAIN LANGUAGE ONLY (Layer-1: the prep doc's
     `list_header` + `request_line_format` verbatim, i.e. exactly "{n}. {title}" per line — NEVER
     technical_trail/hex/paths in the default view). `--details` additionally shows the Layer-3
     drill-in (summary why/impact/reversible + technical_trail + binding.canonical_hash) — an
     explicit opt-in, never the default.
  2. `approve <request_id>` — begins the challenge via the SAME `ApprovalSigner` class P1 built,
     prints the prep doc's `approve_prompt` copy, runs a REAL FIDO2 GetAssertion locally against a
     real hidraw USB security key (`HidrawFido2Client`, python-fido2's `Ctap2`/`Fido2Client` over
     `CtapHidDevice`), and hands that assertion to `ApprovalSigner.sign_request` — the identical
     verification path (allowlist lookup, challenge match, user_verification=required, anti-clone,
     single-use + executed-id ledgers) a browser-driven P2 approval goes through. On success prints
     the prep doc's `success_message`; on ANY failure (no device, wrong/expired challenge, unknown
     or revoked credential, no user verification) prints a plain-language denial and signs NOTHING.
  3. `deny <request_id>` — pending -> denied. No assertion, no signer call.

No-passphrase-fallback (design invariant, PRD R2): there is NO passphrase, key-file, or offline
signing path anywhere in this file as a SUBSTITUTE for the human factor. The only thing this CLI
ever does to obtain proof-of-presence is drive a real hardware FIDO2 ceremony via
`HidrawFido2Client` — an agent invoking this CLI without the physical authenticator present (and,
for `user_verification=required`, without passing its on-device UV check) cannot produce a
signature, full stop; `ApprovalSigner.sign_request` verifies that independently regardless of
anything this CLI does. `--owner-key-path` (below) is NOT a human-factor bypass: it supplies the
service's own Ed25519 SIGNING key (the same `owner_key: bytes` parameter
`approval_signer.ApprovalSigner.sign_request` has always required — see that module's docstring:
"the owner Ed25519 private key is handed in as `owner_key: bytes` by that future service wrapper").
`sign_request` never signs with it until AFTER a verified, fresh, request-bound, user-verified
hardware assertion clears every check; loading it earlier changes nothing about that gate. A
FIDO2 CTAP2 PIN (via `_CliUserInteraction.request_pin`, prompted locally, never stored) is part of
the WebAuthn standard's OWN on-device user-verification mechanism, not a substitute for it: no PIN,
however correct, produces a valid assertion without the physical authenticator present.

SCOPE FENCE (ACP-P4-DESIGN "Scope fence" + task bounds): no systemd/Nix unit, no declarative udev
rule (documented below in `HidrawFido2Client`'s docstring instead — a live deployment must add one
declaratively per Rule 13, NixOS declarative-only), no live request store (`FixtureApprovalStore`
below is an offline placeholder, exactly mirroring the pattern
`dashboard/backend/api/routes/approvals.py`'s P2 build already established with its own
`FixtureApprovalStore`/`configure_store()` seam — a future deployment supplies a real
`ApprovalStore`), no browser UI (P2, already built), no recovery/registration (P1b). Default-OFF:
nothing here is invoked from any live path, service unit, or QA gate.
"""

